or a lot of people it's not  money that actually makes a difference  and if that's the case then we have to  ask well was it worth going through  something so massive an overhaul of the  entire tax system endless amount of time  for our elected officials in the House  and Senate and the president and staff  all for a tiny change from  most people that allows the very rich to  get drastic cuts and what will  eventually be the effective tax rates  that they pay when they file taxes for  2018 next year and that gets to the  other point of the poll in the poll of  those who said we do have more take-home  pay only 38% say that the difference is  enough to even be fairly significant to  them that's not exactly what I would  consider a major major victory so the  biggest question is major tax cuts right  major major tax cuts for the rich tiny  changes for some working-class people by  and large not life-changing hugely  damaging to the economy over the long  term is it worth it I guess we'll see in  a few years and as far as whether it's a  voting issue we'll see in November yeah  I'd imagine for most people it's  something like $20 per pay period or $50  per pay period yeah you know I don't  want to laugh at that no that could be a  lot to someone yeah but for most of us  that think it's kind of negligible it's  kind of touchy to even talk about it  because as soon as you say these are  meaningless amounts of money you  immediately hear the Republicans who  advocated for this saying how elitist of  you to suggest that 20 bucks a pay  period for someone is meaningless for a  lot of people if they're paid every two  weeks an extra $40 a month covers  formula for their baby or whatever the  right Costco membership was the thing  that Paul Ryan or whoever was talking  about we're not saying that $40 a month  is meaningless to everyone what we're  saying is in the grand scheme of how  much money went into this how it might  affect other elements of the economy  over the long 